.TH WT 1 "November 2026" "wt 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wt \- manage Git worktrees from Bash and Zsh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wt
.RI [ global-option ] " command " [ arguments ]
.br
.B wt
.RB [ \-\-help | \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wt
is a shell-based helper for creating, switching between, listing, removing,
and inspecting Git worktrees. It uses Git's native worktree commands and
stores its configuration in
.BR git-config (1).
The executable is written in Bash; optional Bash and Zsh integration changes
the calling shell's directory after navigation commands.
.PP
Most commands operate on the Git repository associated with the current
working directory. In commands with interactive selection, an omitted branch
or worktree name opens
.B fzf
when available, or a numbered terminal menu.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B wt config
Run an interactive configuration wizard, saving settings to global Git
configuration. Blank or whitespace-only input accepts the displayed default:
an existing value in the chosen scope, if present, or a built-in value.
.TP
.B wt config \-\-local
Run the wizard for the current repository rather than the global scope.
.B wt config \-\-global
is equivalent to
.BR "wt config" .
.TP
.B wt config show
Display
.B wt.*
settings stored in the selected configuration scope. This is not a merged
view of effective configuration.
.TP
.BI "wt config get " key
Read a configuration key from the selected scope.
.TP
.BI "wt config set " "key value"
Set a configuration key in the selected scope.
.TP
.BI "wt config unset " key
Remove all values for a configuration key from the selected scope.
.PP
For
.BR show ,
.BR get ,
.BR set ,
and
.BR unset ,
add
.B \-\-local
or
.B \-\-global
after
.B config
or after the action to select the scope. Key names can be written either
as
.B root
or
.BR wt.root .
.TP
.BI "wt clone " "repository " [ destination ]
Clone a repository to an organized directory. A
.B owner/repo
shorthand is interpreted as a GitHub repository; full Git URLs are supported.
By default, the destination is determined by
.B wt.repoRoot
and
.BR wt.repoPattern .
An explicit
.I destination
must be an absolute path and overrides that layout.
.TP
.BI "wt create " "branch " [ base ]
Create a new branch and corresponding worktree. Without
.IR base ,
the starting ref is chosen from the remote default branch, then
.B main
or
.BR master ,
and finally the current branch.
.TP
.BI "wt co " [ branch ]
Check out an existing local or
.B origin
remote branch in a worktree. If it is already checked out in a worktree,
return that worktree's path. When
.I branch
is omitted, select interactively.
.B wt checkout
is an alias.
.TP
.BI "wt cd " [ branch ]
Find an existing worktree without creating one. With shell integration,
change to its directory; otherwise, print its path. Omitting
.I branch
opens an interactive selector.
.B wt sw
and
.B wt switch
are aliases.
.TP
.B wt ls
List worktrees, their branches, and paths, marking the current worktree.
.B wt list
is an alias.
.TP
.B wt status
Show clean/dirty status and available upstream ahead/behind counts for
each worktree.
.TP
.BI "wt rm " [ branch ]
Remove a non-primary worktree while retaining its Git branch. Git refuses
to remove worktrees with uncommitted changes by default. With no
.IR branch ,
select a worktree interactively.
.B wt remove
is an alias.
.TP
.BI "wt pr " [ number | URL ]
Create or enter a worktree for a GitHub pull request using the
.B gh
CLI. With no argument, select an open pull request interactively.
.TP
.BI "wt mr " "number | URL"
Create or enter a worktree for a GitLab merge request using
.B glab
and
.BR jq .
The merge request identifier is required; interactive MR selection
is not implemented.
.TP
.BI "wt copy " [ branch ]
Populate the current or specified worktree with selected untracked/ignored
files and symlinks from the primary worktree. Existing destination files
are not overwritten, and tracked files are not copied.
.TP
.BI "wt migrate " [ \-\-dry\-run ]
Move non-primary worktrees to the directories dictated by the active
strategy and pattern. With
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
print the planned moves without modifying the filesystem.
.TP
.BI "wt cleanup \-\-stale " [ \-\-stale\-days=N ]
Report worktrees whose last commit is older than
.I N
days (default 30) or whose upstream is gone. This command reports
candidates; it does not delete worktrees.
.TP
.BI "wt prune " [ git-worktree-prune-options ]
Pass arguments to
.B git worktree prune
to remove obsolete administrative metadata.
.TP
.B wt info
Show the effective root, strategy, separator, path pattern, clone root,
and clone pattern.
.TP
.B wt version
Print the shell implementation version.
.TP
.BR wt help " | " "wt \-\-help"
Display short usage information.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are processed before the command.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-copy
Skip configured file copying after worktree creation or checkout.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-path
Print the destination path for shell integration. This is primarily
intended for the Bash and Zsh wrappers.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show command usage.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is stored in Git's standard configuration files. Unless
.B \-\-local
is specified,
.B wt config
writes to the global scope. During normal operations,
.B git config
provides its usual precedence, including local repository settings
over global settings.
.PP
Run
.B wt config
to configure the options interactively. To change one option directly:
.PP
.nf
.RS
wt config set strategy sibling-repo
git config --global wt.separator -
git config --local --add wt.copy .env
.RE
.fi
.PP
Supported configuration keys:
.TP
.B wt.root
Root for worktrees using the
.B global
strategy. Default:
.BR ~/.worktrees .
.TP
.B wt.strategy
Directory placement strategy. Default:
.BR global .
.TP
.B wt.pattern
Custom worktree path template. When set, this overrides the built-in
template for any strategy. Required for the
.B custom
strategy.
.TP
.B wt.separator
Replacement for slashes in branch names, such as
.B /
(default),
.BR \- ,
or
.BR _ .
The interactive wizard accepts these three values.
.TP
.B wt.repoRoot
Root directory for cloned repositories. Default:
.BR ~/dev/repos .
.TP
.B wt.repoPattern
Clone directory template. Default:
.BR "{.repoRoot}/{.repo.Host}/{.repo.Owner}/{.repo.Name}/{.branch}" .
.TP
.B wt.copy
Repeatable pattern for untracked or ignored files to copy to a new
worktree. Add multiple values with
.BR "git config --add" .
.TP
.B wt.link
Repeatable path to symlink from the primary worktree into new worktrees.
.TP
.B wt.exclude
Repeatable pattern excluding files from population.
.TP
.B wt.copyIgnored
If
.BR true ,
attempt to copy all ignored files. Default:
.BR false .
.PP
A
.B .worktreeinclude
file in the primary worktree can also list copy patterns, one per line.
Pattern matching is based on shell globs and is not a complete
implementation of Git's gitignore syntax.
.SH WORKTREE STRATEGIES
The directory layouts below assume the primary checkout is
.B ~/dev/repo
and the branch is
.BR feature .
.TP
.B global
.BR ~/.worktrees/repo/feature
.TP
.B sibling-repo
.BR ~/dev/repo-feature
.TP
.B parent-branches
.BR ~/dev/feature
.TP
.B parent-worktrees
.BR ~/dev/repo.worktrees/feature
.TP
.B parent-dotdir
.BR ~/dev/.worktrees/feature
.TP
.B inside-dotdir
.BR ~/dev/repo/.worktrees/feature
.TP
.B custom
Use the value of
.BR wt.pattern .
.PP
Worktree templates recognize
.BR "{.worktreeRoot}" ,
.BR "{.repo.Main}" ,
.BR "{.repo.Name}" ,
.BR "{.branch}" ,
and environment substitutions
.B "{.env.NAME}"
or
.BR "{.env.NAME:-fallback}" .
Clone templates also recognize
.BR "{.repoRoot}" ,
.BR "{.repo.Host}" ,
and
.BR "{.repo.Owner}" .
These are a subset of the original Go program's template variables.
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
Installing the executable alone is sufficient for Git worktree operations,
but a subprocess cannot change the working directory of its parent shell.
To make
.BR "wt cd" ,
.BR "wt co" ,
and other navigation commands change directories, source the appropriate
wrapper in the shell startup file:
.PP
.B Bash
.RS
.nf
source /path/to/wt/shell/wt.bash
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Zsh
.RS
.nf
source /path/to/wt/shell/wt.zsh
.fi
.RE
.PP
The wrappers provide shell-specific completion as well.
.SH INSTALLATION
Copy the
.B wt
executable into a directory on
.BR PATH .
Install this manual as
.B wt.1
in a directory searched by the system's manual-page reader, such as
.BR ~/.local/share/man/man1/ .
.PP
.nf
.RS
mkdir -p ~/.local/bin ~/.local/share/man/man1
install -m 755 wt ~/.local/bin/wt
install -m 644 man/wt.1 ~/.local/share/man/man1/wt.1
man wt
.RE
.fi
.PP
If the local manual directory is not searched automatically, add
.B ~/.local/share/man
to
.B MANPATH
or view the file directly using
.BR "man -l man/wt.1" .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
wt config
wt create feature-login
wt co feature-login
wt cd main
wt rm feature-login
wt pr 123
wt status
wt migrate --dry-run
wt cleanup --stale --stale-days=14
.RE
.fi
.SH SAFETY AND LIMITATIONS
.B wt
rejects numerous unsafe destination paths, including Git metadata,
existing worktrees, and selected sensitive user/system configuration
locations. It validates symlink-resolved destinations before performing
filesystem operations.
.PP
Failed or interrupted worktree creation attempts to remove the newly
registered worktree; a newly created branch is removed only if its
commit has not changed. Migration attempts to reverse earlier moves if
a later move fails.
.PP
These protections are not atomic. Concurrent changes to filesystem paths
and failed rollback operations may still require manual intervention.
Avoid using worktree patterns that target sensitive directories.
.PP
This implementation intentionally omits hooks, hook trust, TOML, Fish
integration, JSON output, and the original Go version's CI dashboard.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero on success and nonzero on an error. A failure during a
multi-step filesystem operation may report that manual cleanup is needed.
